# Laboratory 04: The Cloud-Native Engineer

## Mission Overview

CloudNova Technologies has a client whose traditional Virtual Machines boot slowly and waste RAM. In this mission, I compared VMs with containers, then used a KillerCoda Docker playground to deploy, manage, and remove a containerized Nginx web server, documenting every command so the client's IT team can replicate it.

## Objectives

- Differentiate between traditional Virtual Machines (VMs) and Containers.
- Access a Docker-enabled cloud environment using KillerCoda.
- Execute fundamental Docker CLI commands.
- Pull, run, manage, and terminate a containerized application (Nginx).
- Create professional technical documentation using Markdown.
- Continue developing a well-organized GitHub Cloud Computing Portfolio.

## Docker Commands Executed

| Checkpoint | Command | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| 3 | `docker --version` | Verify Docker is installed |
| 3 | `docker info` | Check the status of the Docker environment |
| 4 | `docker pull nginx` | Download the official Nginx image from Docker Hub |
| 4 | `docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name my-nginx nginx` | Run Nginx in detached mode, mapping host port 8080 to container port 80 |
| 4 | `curl http://localhost:8080` | Confirm the web server responds |
| 5 | `docker ps` | List running containers |
| 5 | `docker stop my-nginx` | Stop the running container |
| 5 | `docker ps -a` | Verify the container is stopped |
| 5 | `docker rm my-nginx` | Remove the container completely |

## Skills Learned

- Explaining the architectural differences between VMs and containers.
- Verifying Docker installation and status on Linux.
- Pulling images and running containers in detached mode.
- Exposing a container's service through port mapping.
- Managing the container lifecycle (run, list, stop, remove).
- Documenting technical procedures in Markdown and organizing a GitHub repository.

## Challenges Encountered

- Remembering the direction of port mapping (`host:container`, so `8080:80`).
- Understanding the difference between `docker ps` and `docker ps -a`.
- Learning that a container must be stopped before it can be removed with `docker rm`.
- Taking clear screenshots of the KillerCoda terminal.

